  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Using 99 audit negatives may change the training objective. If baseline training uses one negative per positive, a joint 99-negative loss changes weighting and potentially coupling. Specify whether the audit averages 99 baseline-equivalent pair losses or evaluates a counterfactual objective; these answer different questions.",
    "Small score derivatives do not alone establish harmful saturation. For BCE, confident correct predictions naturally have small derivatives. Report positive and negative contributions separately, distinguish correct from incorrect predictions, and compare supplied training negatives with audit negatives before proposing harder sampling.",
    "Feature-gradient norms depend on scorer parameterization. For a dot-product scorer, reciprocally rescaling features and item embeddings preserves scores but changes feature-gradient norms. Separate positive and negative gradient vectors and their cancellation; avoid interpreting aggregate norms as scale-independent optimization quality.",
    "Scorer reproduction needs an explicit equivalence check against view.predict_score, including biases, temperatures, normalization and learned transformations. Detached-feature gradients exclude backbone Jacobians, tied-embedding pathways and optimizer effects, so they cannot establish actual parameter-update strength.",
    "Reproducibility remains incomplete: define prefix-target selection, repeated-item handling, negative exclusions and sampling distribution, undersized-pool behavior, frequency-bin boundaries, aggregation denominators, numerical precision, and a concrete runtime cap. The actual objective and scorer require supplied-code evidence; literature cannot establish these implementation facts.",
    "Originality 4/10 is plausible, but potential 7/10 remains speculative without evidence that the audit informs successful validation interventions. Difficulty 4/10 may understate faithful objective/scorer integration. The existing candidate-depth audit should be completed and summarized before adding another diagnostic layer."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Clarify how to preserve the baseline learning objective when reusing a much larger candidate set, and distinguish negative-count effects from easy-negative saturation.",
      "query": "In sequential recommendation trained with sampled binary cross-entropy or BPR using one negative per positive, how should a diagnostic over 99 unique negatives preserve the original loss weighting and gradient expectation? Compare averaging baseline-equivalent pair losses with joint multi-negative BCE and sampled softmax, including negative-sampling distribution corrections and gSASRec's overconfidence analysis."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Identify controls needed to interpret local gradients without confusing parameter scale, gradient cancellation or omitted backbone pathways with weak learning.",
      "query": "For tied-embedding dot-product sequential recommenders such as NextItNet, how do reciprocal feature/item-embedding rescaling, positive-negative gradient cancellation, and the encoder Jacobian affect detached-feature gradient norms? What established diagnostics distinguish small logit derivatives from small full-parameter gradients or optimizer updates?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether this diagnostic adds actionable evidence beyond existing training margins and candidate ranks, while keeping any subsequent experiment within one NextItNet model per category.",
      "query": "What empirical recommender-system studies show that measured easy-negative gradient saturation predicts held-out ranking gains from semi-hard negative sampling? Examine controls for false negatives, popularity bias, fitted-training examples, and fixed sampled HR@10 evaluation, including gSASRec and Evaluating Performance and Bias of Negative Sampling."
    }
  ]
}
